    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "67b63b4f-a2ec-586f-864c-81bdd436e7fe",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Margaret MacDonald, James Campbell, Isabella Campbell, Mary Campbell, James MacDonald, George MacDonald, Edward Irving, Henry Drummond, John Nelson Darby, Benjamin Wills Newton\n- Organizations: Plymouth Brethren, Exclusive Brethren\n- Places: Rosneath\n- Concepts: charismatic experiences, speaking in tongues, interpretations of tongues, miraculous healings, Holy Spirit, demonic displays, pre-tribulation rapture theology\n\nFacts:\n- Around 1830, miraculous healings were reported through James Campbell, first involving his sister Margaret MacDonald and then Mary Campbell through James Campbell's letter to Mary Campbell.\n- In Rosneath, Isabella Campbell and Mary Campbell manifested charismatic experiences such as speaking in tongues.\n- Shortly after the reported healings, James MacDonald and George MacDonald manifested speaking in tongues and interpretations of tongues, and others soon did the same in prayer meetings.\n- These charismatic experiences drew major national attention, and many people came to see and investigate them.\n- Edward Irving and Henry Drummond regarded these events as genuine manifestations of the Holy Spirit.\n- John Nelson Darby and Benjamin Wills Newton, sent by the Plymouth Brethren to investigate, concluded that the displays were demonic.\n- John Nelson Darby, born on 18 November 1800 and died on 29 April 1882, was an Anglo-Irish Bible teacher, a leading figure among the original Plymouth Brethren, and the founder of the Exclusive Brethren.\n- In the 1830s, John Nelson Darby and the Plymouth Brethren extensively popularized pre-tribulation rapture theology, and the Scofield Reference Bible further popularized it in the United States in the early 20th century.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "8aaf5773-39e3-5c77-91a3-0f7295c0c93d",
        "text": "Page: Margaret MacDonald (visionary). Sentence: In response, Isabella and Mary Campbell of the parish of Rosneath manifested charismatic experiences such as speaking in tongues.\nPage: Margaret MacDonald (visionary). Sentence: Around 1830, miraculous healings were reported through James Campbell, first of his sister Margaret MacDonald and then of Mary Campbell (through James's letter to Mary).\nPage: Margaret MacDonald (visionary). Sentence: Shortly thereafter, James and George MacDonald manifested the speaking and interpretations of tongues, and soon others followed suit in prayer meetings.\nPage: Margaret MacDonald (visionary). Sentence: These charismatic experiences garnered major national attention.\nPage: Margaret MacDonald (visionary). Sentence: Many came to see and investigate these events.\nPage: Margaret MacDonald (visionary). Sentence: Some, such as Edward Irving and Henry Drummond, regarded these events as genuine displays from the Holy Spirit.\nPage: Margaret MacDonald (visionary). Sentence: Others, including John Nelson Darby and Benjamin Wills Newton, whom the Plymouth Brethren sent on their behalf to investigate, came to the conclusion that these displays were demonic.\nPage: John Nelson Darby. Sentence: John Nelson Darby (18 November 1800 – 29 April 1882) was an Anglo-Irish Bible teacher, one of the influential figures among the original Plymouth Brethren and the founder of the Exclusive Brethren.\nPage: John Nelson Darby. Sentence: Pre-tribulation rapture theology was popularized extensively in the 1830s by John Nelson Darby and the Plymouth Brethren, and further popularized in the United States in the early 20th century by the wide circulation of the Scofield Reference Bible.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
